<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaga Province</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">823–1871</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaga Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> coast, now the southern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishikawa-prefecture">Ishikawa Prefecture</a>. From 1488 to 1580 it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a> leagues of True Pure Land believers, and contemporaries called it a province held by peasants. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> it formed the core of the Kaga domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda clan</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kaga-province.jpg" alt="Kaga Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Kaga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
